On Sat, 26 Apr 2003 23:30:00 +0200, Frank Mittelbach wrote:

>After talking with Walter at the DANTE meeting in Bremen we came to the
>conclusion that perhaps it isn't such a bad idea to make TS1 safe for =
users
>(or at least he gave in on me :-)

Indeed, the need for yet another configuration file
(textcomp.cfg) does not make me happy.  Now that I see the
actual implmentation, I really doubt whether all of this is
a good and useful idea.


> - is there a point in having a logic that even numbers mean a matching =
subset
>   while odd numbers mean that the subset is not complete?=20
>   in that case one
>   could have a warning if a potential dangerous font is used

The average user either ignores LaTeX warnings or regards
them as confusing -- this is my experience.  Any user, who
would really understand the warning, does not actually need
it.  The use of the warning is not worth the effort.
IMO, good documentation on textcomp and its problems with
font from the non-TeX world would be sufficient.  Presently,
there is no documentation at all!


> - is there a point in the generalisation implemented that would allow =
using
>   this even for other font encodings, eg T1?

Oh no!  The use isn't worth the effort.  And I guess it=20
would slow doen LaTeX.


 - i left some of the options from earlier implementations, right now =
you can
   define the "default" [...]

IMHO, the default subset should be {1} rather than {5}, to
make sure that any "non-standard" symbols (if available) can
be used in additional fonts, without having to add the font
family name to textcomp.cfg.  With the default being {5}, I
will be flooded with questions such as "I have installed the
font family xyz using the latest fontinst.  Why can't I use
the Euro symbols?" Installing additional fonts is already=20
very difficult.  Users should not be _forced_ to edit also
textcomp.cfg, just to access all existing symbols in a font.


> - if i understand Walter's list correctly then the Micropress fonts =
are
>   incomplete

They have more glyphs than just subset #3, but they don't
provide all of TS1; see the attached sample of BA-Math.
Oops, I just see that they provide even \textcircled, so we
must assign #0, rather than #1.

It comes to my mind, that the idea behind the special
handling of \textcircled and \t in was to take them from=20
the OMS and OML encodings, when textcomp has been loaded=20
with an appropriate option.  In this case, textcomp would=20
not overwrite the existing definitions which point to OMS=20
and OML.  Now that the character set is defined on a=20
"per font" base, rather than through a package option, this
cannot be implemented any longer.  Maybe we should give up
#1 entirely.


> - anything else obviously strange or wrong?

The oldstyle numbers just don't fit into the scheme...


Finally, let me repeat a desciption of the TS1 subsets which
I had proposed.  The comments in textcomp.cfg are not very
detailed and they are lacking my reasong (and they are in
German...).  I refer to the numbering used in Frank's new
textcomp.sty now:

#5 =3D those TS1 symbols only, that are also in the ISO-Adobe
character set; without "currency", which is often misused for=20
the Euro.  Older Type1 fonts from the non-TeX world provide=20
only this subset.

#4 =3D #5 + "Euro"
Most newer fonts provide this.

#3 =3D #4 + "Omega"
Can also be described as TS1 \cap (ISO-Adobe \cup MacRoman).
(Except for the missing "currency".)

#2 =3D  #3 + "estimated" + "currency"
Can also be described as TS1 \cap Adobe-Western-2. =20
This may be relevant for OpenType fonts,=20
which usually show the Adobe-Western-2 charecter set.

#1 =3D TS1 without \textcircled and \t.
Should perhaps be given up; see above.

#0 =3D full TS1


best wishes
Walter


